\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1 We identify conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) as a systematic failure mode of temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training. Trained $\dt$-embeddings with multi-rate data exposure reliably induce value overestimation while frozen embeddings never do, revealing co-adaptation between the temporal embedding and the world model as the causal factor. The NVE diagnostic achieves perfect classification across all tested conditions (AUROC${=}1.000$, $n{=}36$). A four-level intervention hierarchy yields two key insights: gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance (the dominant pathway is causally inert while the weakest drives CIVO), and dual critics converge in pure imagination, rendering Double Q pessimism structurally ineffective in any system with shared imagined rollouts.

\looseness=-1 For practitioners, we recommend: (1)~validate with frozen-embedding controls before deploying learned embeddings; (2)~monitor NVE as an early warning; (3)~prefer MPC-style re-planning for variable-rate execution. Future work should examine whether CIVO manifests in transformer-based world models and diffusion-based planners; the wide dual-critic result suggests architectural solutions exist but require deeper investigation. Code and experiment configurations will be released upon acceptance.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\looseness=-1 All experiments use DreamerV3's RSSM; generalization to transformer-based world models \citep{micheli2023iris}, TD-MPC variants \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}, or diffusion-based planners is untested. The evaluation covers two locomotion and one manipulation environment with proprioceptive observations; pixel-based or real-robot settings remain open. Seed counts (4--5; \citealt{colas2018seeds}) provide sufficient statistical power for our binary classification but may miss subtler, more gradual, or environment-specific effects.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
\looseness=-1 CIVO propagates through multiple pathways: Walker Walk develops a $1.63\times$ reward prediction gap while Cheetah Run shows none ($1.00\times$) despite higher severity ($8.27\times$ NVE). The mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced; unlike MPC, imagination-based actor-critic bootstraps value targets entirely within the learned model without real-data correction (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}). Untrained embeddings at $\dt{=}2$ produce no overestimation (NVE${<}0.6\times$), confirming \emph{systematic} bias. Frozen embeddings participate identically in the forward pass yet never trigger CIVO (0/16 controls vs.\ 5/7 trained, $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}): the vulnerability lies in what the embedding \emph{learns}, not in conditioning architecture.

\paragraph{Why interventions fail or partially succeed.}
\looseness=-1 CIVO is an emergent property of co-adapting embeddings, not a single-pathway defect: SGS fails from pathway redundancy; RAVE fails as the EMA anchor tracks biased values; TCN shows binary on/off behavior suggesting a phase transition. The only reliable elimination, freezing the embedding (0/16 controls), degrades performance to 22--33\% of baseline. At the architecture level, the $2{\times}2$ critic factorial shows that min-aggregation enables recovery but not prevention; their combination achieves prevention in 3/4 seeds (NVE $\leq 1.4\times$), though Cheetah transfer failure (0/1, CIVO at $70\times$) suggests a severity ceiling. CIVO is specific to multi-joint locomotion across both tested environments (5/7 seeds; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_env_appendix}).

\paragraph{Implications beyond DreamerV3.}
\looseness=-1 The gradient-magnitude$\neq$causal-importance dissociation and dual-critic convergence failure are not architecture-specific; they may arise in any imagination-based actor-critic that bootstraps value targets within a learned model. Embedding co-adaptation, rather than forward-pass conditioning, drives the vulnerability, suggesting that representation learning and value estimation may interact pathologically in other latent-imagination frameworks.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
\label{sec:detector}
\looseness=-1 Three guidelines follow. (1)~Monitor NVE throughout training: a sustained rise above $2.0\times$ indicates CIVO onset (online detector: TPR~$5/5$, FPR~$2/9$; Algorithm~\ref{alg:civo_detector}, Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}). (2)~Use frozen-embedding runs as a diagnostic control: if frozen matches trained, the embedding fuels co-adaptation rather than temporal reasoning. (3)~Wide dual-critic (min $+$ capacity) is the only partial prevention (3/4); scaling this approach or decoupling the embedding from value prediction represent promising directions for future research.

